WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.979 --> 00:00:05.279
The workbook scenario called for having a
person enter two numbers and then return a

00:00:05.279 --> 00:00:11.160
random calculation for addition, subtraction,
or multiplication on the two numbers, using

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:14.460
a specific format for the statements returned.

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:19.350
Here is the code example I did to satisfy
the need for the scenario: knowing I would

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:25.800
need random numbers, the code on line 1 imports
the random library.

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:34.490
Lines 2 and 3 use inputs, converted to integers,
to store two numbers in two variables.

00:00:34.490 --> 00:00:44.100
Then, on line 5, a function is defined using
the two numbers inputted as arguments.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:51.129
On line 6, I use the randint method to get
a random number between 1 and 3.

00:00:51.129 --> 00:01:00.200
Then, on lines 7 through 15, we have an if
statement that checks the number and adds

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:08.390
the numbers if the random number is 1, subtracts
the numbers if the random number is 2, and,

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:15.930
if the number is 3, as indicated with the
else on line 13, the two numbers are multiplied.

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:22.960
Regarding the print statements on lines 9,
12, and 15, I used the comma to do all the

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:26.950
concatenation so that I do not need to worry
about spacing.

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:31.590
You can also use an f-string format or string.format
on these print statements to get the same

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:32.799
effect.

00:01:32.799 --> 00:01:38.409
Lastly, I call the calculate function on line
17.

00:01:38.409 --> 00:01:51.579
I will run this code a few times, and we see
the calculations and the statements that we

00:01:51.579 --> 00:01:52.579
want.

00:01:52.579 --> 00:01:57.219
Again, there is more than one way to write
the code necessary for this project.

00:01:57.219 --> 00:02:02.369
As we bring this course to a close, I recommend
getting plenty of practice with the topics

00:02:02.369 --> 00:02:04.520
we covered in this course.

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:09.640
Get plenty of practice looking at existing
code and analyzing code to figure out what

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:13.630
the code will do, as we have done many times
in this course.

00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:18.560
Make code examples relate to something related
to you, as we have for gaming examples in

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:19.989
this course.

00:02:19.989 --> 00:02:24.910
Finally, take advantage of the practice tests
and extra practice opportunities we have provided

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:26.450
in this course.

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:31.340
Thanks again for taking this course, and best
of luck to you in your future endeavors.

